   529	**Why one buffer set, not two generations (Claude review R1).** In the canonical loop, step t+1 needs actions from the forward on obs t, and that forward consumes the H2D copy of obs t. A second generation therefore buys no overlap. It would cost a second pinned buffer set, a full-row copy on every truncate, a new trainer API at every copy site, and a change to Isaiah's truncate plumbing (:991). The contract allows a synchronous baseline ("A synchronous-copy baseline satisfies this", W:docs/kaggriculture-contract.md, Environment). R1 makes that baseline explicit with the entry fence. Reopen double buffering only if a measured profile shows native step overlapping with device work would pay (Phase 6 Nsight), and then with the event design Codex drafted (preserved in commit `6ea11d2`, Codex's draft of this brief).
